  "background": "#e1e1e1",
  "content": "<!-- <lightimg src=\"https://s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/codex-content/uploads/images/heads2.jpg\" width=\"2072\" height=\"1383\"></lightimg> -->\n<lightimg src=\"https://codex-content.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/images/1534471962908-server.jpg\" width=\"2400\" height=\"2949\"></lightimg>\n<div class=\"indents\">    \n    <p>HPE ProLiant DL380’s nostrils dilated as it assiduously surveyed and switched packets. It couldn’t help it. It was about to die; it had better make this count. Capable, though more <em>liant</em> than lion, it had already died many thousands of times since 0000 UTC. It could also slide like a trundle bed.</p>\n    <p>A signal ran through its not-unhappiness: a request had fluttered their eyelids, the hewn form of which mirrored the short bolts that held up all HPE ProLiant DL380s by their sides arthritically. A look was between us. The nature of the thing was not to ask for anything, instead treating suddenness as immanence.\n    </p><p>The look of the request arrived with a crush at the server’s outermost and thick service layer, produced from a <aa href=\"10,4\">pathology of convention</aa> and availability. The payload of your eyes is a English-language question about how much effort I exert when I sleep. Something like this doesn’t require parsing, only matching, realised the HPE. \n    </p>\n</div>",
